Twine, the Video-Game Technology for All by Laura Hudson was interesting because it demonstrates that creativity does not require a lot of money, or sponsorship by big corporations/streamers. Although it could be challenging, if the individual focuses on the outcome and its impact it could have on society, it is worth it. For example Twine, it is an accessible platform where people can share stories through video-game. This was a point of interest because it shows that game development doesn’t need to be expensive and with complicated programming instead it could be simple that encourages people of all ages to participate and interact with it. Another point of interest was that the games don’t have to be violent and random all the time, but they could be of a personal experience where people would have a voice and narrative. This proven when the author says, “Twine games look and feel profoundly different from other games, not just because they’re made with
different tools but also because they’re made by different people.” 
My take aways from Twine, the Video-Game Technology for All by Laura Hudson was that programming could be used as a tool to express people's feelings and share personal stories as games so people could feel similar emotions. Also, not everyone created programming for money, but to influence people because at one time the creator of Twine didn't want to eat at restaurant because she is on a budget. 

